course: everyday
lesson: 372
title: Jake, always in bruises
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Jake turned up on Monday with a purple bruise on his cheek. By Wednesday Jake had told different people four different stories about it: a fight, a goalpost, a bike and, to the Year 2s, a bear. Mrs Okafor quietly asked which one was true. It was the goalpost. Jake had been proud of the bruise, but secretly it was sore and he was fed up with it.
explain: Jake's mum keeps a box just for him: plasters, arnica cream (a cream for bruises) and a bag of frozen peas. Jake thought it was funny, until one evening his mum cried while holding the peas on his eye. That hurt him more than the eye did. Every bruise is also somebody else's worry.
explain: The Year 3s hid behind the climbing frame whenever Jake walked past, and everyone picked him first at football because they were scared not to. It felt powerful, then oddly lonely, because nobody passed him the ball either. Being feared is not the same as being liked. When Jake helped a little one get a ball off the roof, they stopped hiding and asked him to play in goal.
explain: Jake had other battles. When nobody was looking, he asked Sam for help with spelling, embarrassed that a tough boy needed it; after some sessions with Sam at the library, his spellings went from 3 out of 10 to 8. A supply teacher called him «a troublemaker» before he had done anything, so Jake behaved perfectly all day to prove her wrong. And when Jake and Maddie compared bruises at lunch like stickers, Lily said «that's actually really sad», because bruises are injuries, not a collection to be proud of.
explain: After losing a match, Jake punched a wall and broke his wrist: six weeks in a cast, no football, no writing, and the wall was completely fine. Worse, his little brother Ben started copying him and got into his first fight in Year 2. When Jake told him fighting was stupid, Ben said «but you do it», and Jake had no answer.
fact: A bruise is bleeding under the skin; it changes colour from purple to green to yellow as the body clears it.
fact: A bruise is an injury, not a medal, even when people talk about it like one.
fact: The people who look after an injured child feel worried and tired every time it happens.
fact: People who are feared are often lonely, because fear keeps others away.
fact: Being picked because people are scared is not the same as being wanted.
fact: Punching a wall can break bones in the hand or wrist; the wall is not hurt at all.
fact: Younger children copy what older children do more than what they say.
fact: Anger needs a safe outlet, like running hard, a pillow or talking.
fact: Asking for help with schoolwork is a strong thing to do, not a soft one.
fact: A bad reputation can arrive before you, but it can be changed day by day.
exercise: Why were there four different stories about Jake's bruise? | Jake told a different story to different people | Four different doctors looked at it | It changed colour four times a day | Four different boys had hit him
exercise: What really caused the Monday bruise? | A goalpost | A wild bear | A fight behind the shops | A fall from his bike
exercise: How did Jake really feel about the bruise underneath? | Sore and fed up, even though he acted proud | Completely happy about it | He did not notice it at all | Angry with the goalpost
exercise: What is in the box Jake's mum keeps for him? | Plasters, arnica cream and frozen peas | Sweets and comics to cheer him up | Football cards | Spare school uniform
exercise: What made Jake feel worse than his sore eye? | His mum crying while she held the peas on it | Missing his favourite programme on TV that evening | The peas being cold | His brother laughing
exercise: The Year 3s hid whenever Jake walked past. How did that come to feel for Jake? | Powerful at first, then lonely | Lonely at first, then very powerful | Exciting every single time it happened | It did not matter to him at all
exercise: What made the little ones stop hiding from Jake? | He helped one of them get a ball off the roof | He shouted at them to stop | He gave them his lunch | He stopped coming to school
exercise: Why was being picked first at football not as good as it looked? | People picked him out of fear, not because they wanted him | He always scored far too many goals and the others got bored | He was always put in the wrong team | He did not like football
exercise: Jake asked Sam for help with spelling when nobody was looking. Why did he hide it? | He was embarrassed that a tough boy needed help | Spelling homework is meant to be secret | Sam told him to hide it | The teacher said not to ask
exercise: What happened to Jake's spellings after meeting Sam at the library? | They went from 3 out of 10 to 8 | They stayed at 3 out of 10 all term | They dropped to zero, because Sam got them all mixed up | He stopped doing his spellings at all
exercise: What did punching the wall cost Jake? | A broken wrist and six weeks without football or writing | A small graze on his knuckles that healed by the next morning | Nothing, as walls are soft | A new wall for the school
exercise: What is a safer place for that kind of anger? | Running hard or punching a pillow | Hitting a door instead of a wall, because doors are softer | Kicking the next person who walks past | Keeping it all inside him for ever
exercise: Ben said «but you do it». What does that show? | Little ones copy what you do, not what you say | Ben was being rude for no reason | Ben did not understand words | Fighting runs in families and cannot change
exercise: A supply teacher called Jake «a troublemaker» before he had done anything. What did Jake do? | Behaved perfectly all day to prove her wrong | Started a fight straight away to prove her right | Went home early | Refused to speak all day
exercise: Jake and Maddie compared bruises like stickers. Lily said «that's actually really sad». Why? | Bruises are injuries, not a collection to be proud of | Lily wanted some bruises of her own to compare | Stickers are better than bruises | Lily was jealous of them
exercise: Which is a kind of courage Jake showed in this lesson? | Asking Sam for help with his spelling | Punching the wall | Telling four different stories about how he got a bruise | Being picked first out of fear
test: «Jake's got a massive shiner, he must've won a fight, legend». What's more likely true? | A bruise only shows someone got hurt, not that they won | A bruise always means you won the fight, whatever happened | Only winners get bruises | Bruises mean you are the strongest
test: «Mum's crying again cos of my eye, it's not even that bad, why's she upset». Best answer? | She worries every time you get hurt | She is really upset about something else at work, not your eye | Mums like crying | She is cross the peas are gone
test: Year 3s scatter when you walk past. Your mate says «that's respect, mate». Is it? | No, that's fear, and fear keeps people away | Yes, that's real respect | Yes, it means all of them like you a lot and want to be your friends | It means they want to play
test: «he punched a wall after we lost lol, wall 1 Jake 0». What's the real lesson? | Walls always win; anger needs a safer place to go | Walls are fine to punch if you're angry | He should have punched a softer wall | Losing a match means you must go and hit something hard
test: Your little brother gets in a fight and says «you do it all the time». What's best? | Admit it and change what you do, not just what you say | Tell him he's far too young to be getting into fights at all | Tell Mum he's lying | Show him how to win next time
test: Supply teacher: «I've heard all about you, troublemaker». You've done nothing. What's the wisest move? | Show her she's wrong by how you act today | Prove her right since she already thinks it | Walk straight out of the classroom in a huff | Tell her what you've heard people say about her
test: «a tough lad like you doesn't need spelling help». Is that right? | No, asking for help is strong, not soft | Yes, tough people already know how to spell everything | Yes, spelling is for Sam only | Only if nobody finds out
test: «everyone picks Jake first so he must be the best player». What else could be true? | They might be scared not to pick him | They are all his cousins | He pays them to pick him | He is the only one in the class who has proper football boots
test: A bruise that started purple is now yellow-green a week later. What is happening? | The body is slowly clearing the bruise | The bruise is getting worse and spreading | It is a new bruise on top | The skin underneath has caught a nasty infection
test: «yours is bigger, mine's darker» — Jake and Maddie at lunch with their bruises. What did Lily see that they didn't? | That being proud of being hurt is really sad | That their bruises were not real | That bruises look nice | That they should both go and get more bruises to win
test: Jake helped a little one get a ball off the roof. Next day the Year 3s asked him to play in goal. Why? | Kindness made them stop being scared of him | They wanted him to climb up and fall off the roof | They had nobody else at all | He told them they had to
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the distinction between being feared and being esteemed? | Fear causes others to keep their distance, whereas esteem draws them nearer | Fear and esteem are entirely identical in their effects upon the conduct of others | Fear is the principal foundation of friendship | Esteem may be obtained only through physical strength
exam: Ascertain the consequence that most commonly follows the striking of a wall with a closed fist in anger. | Injury to the bones of the hand or wrist, the wall remaining undamaged | Damage to the wall, the hand remaining uninjured | The complete and lasting dissipation of all the anger which was felt by the person | No consequence of any kind
exam: It is given that a younger sibling commences fighting after observing an elder sibling do so. Identify the principal reason. | Younger children imitate conduct more readily than they heed instruction | Fighting is transmitted from one generation to another by inheritance alone | Younger children are invariably more aggressive | The younger sibling was instructed to fight
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning a bruise. | It is an injury involving bleeding beneath the skin, and not a distinction | It is evidence of victory in a contest | It indicates strength of character | It is incapable of causing any distress to others, such as parents or siblings
